          ],
          timeline: [
            '1830s: Seven Up becomes the standard name in American gaming parlors.',
            '1860s: Civil War soldiers popularize the game in military camps.',
            "1900s: Gradually evolves into faster variants like 'Pitch' (Auction Pitch).",
          ],
          evolution: "The transition from an 11-point target to a 7-point target (hence 'Seven Up') made the game ideal for quick matches in commercial settings.",
          cultural: "Often associated with the 'Old West'. Winning a game of Seven Up was a common theme in period literature and tall tales.",
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        setup: {
          players: '2 or 4 (Partnerships opposite each other).',
          deck: 'Standard 52-card deck. Ace is high.',
          equipment: 'Standard deck, 7 chips per player/team for scoring.',
          dealing: 'Dealer gives 6 cards to each player (traditionally 3 at a time). The 13th card is turned up for trumps.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Score 7 points by winning High, Low, Jack, and Game in several hands.',
          gameplay: "1. **The Turn-Up**: Dealer flips a card for trumps. 2. **The Beg**: Non-dealer (Eldest) can either 'Stand' (accept trump) or 'Beg'. 3. **The Gift/Run**: If they beg, dealer can: \n   - 'Gift' 1 point to the opponent and keep the trump suit.\n   - OR 'Run the cards': Deal 3 more cards to each player. Flip a new trump. (If the new suit is the same as the first, keep running 3 at a time until a different suit appears).\n4. **Discarding**: If the cards were run, players discard down to exactly 6 cards before play starts. 5. **Play**: Follow suit or trump. 6. **Scoring**: Points awarded in order: High, Low, Jack, Game.",
          keyRules: [
            'High (1 pt): Held by the player dealt the highest trump.',
            'Low (1 pt): Held by the player dealt the lowest trump.',
            'Jack (1 pt): Awarded to the team that WINS the trick containing the Jack of trumps.',
            "Game (1 pt): Awarded to the team with the most 'pips' from captured tricks (A=4, K=3, Q=2, J=1, 10=10).",
            'Game Tie: If the pip count is tied, the point goes to the non-dealing side (Eldest).',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Always protect your 10s. Since they are worth 10 points for 'Game', losing one can forfeit the Game point entirely.",
          intermediate: "The Discard: When running the cards and discarding to 6, throw away your low cards in non-trump suits to increase your chances of being 'void' and thus able to trump early.",
          advanced: "Dealer Strategy: If an opponent begs and you hold the King or Jack of trumps, strongly consider 'Gifting' the 1 point. It's often safer than risking a new trump suit where they might find the Ace.",
          tips: [
            'Low belongs to the holder. If you have the 2 of trumps, it is yours even if you lose that trick.',
            "In 4-player games, leading a small trump can smoke out the opponent's Jack if your partner holds the Ace.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
